tter because:\n- It uses a high card when I still have options.\n- If it wins, I can follow up with smaller cards.\n- If it loses, opponent plays an Ace — which might help break up their hand, but I still have other cards.\n\nBut wait — after I played the rocket, both passed. That suggests they may be low on power cards. Possibly, they don’t have safe leads.\n\nSo leading a high card now may win outright.\n\nMoreover, the previous player has 15 cards — way more than ideal. They are likely stuck with bad combinations.\n\nNext player has 9 — also many, but less.\n\nSo maybe they are both struggling.\n\nTherefore, assert control.\n\nFinal decision: **Play ♣K**\n\nReason: As the landlord aiming to empty my hand first, I should seize control early in a new trick by leading a high card. The ♣K is the highest-ranking single card in my hand, and since no 2s or jokers remain, it can only be beaten by an Ace. Given the opponents\' large hand sizes, forcing them to use high cards benefits me. Playing it now maximizes pressure and potential for winning the trick, allowing me to lead again.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♣K"]}'}
